                             |   Management LIM       | (Master Load Information Manager)
                             | (Elected Head Daemon)  |
                             +-----------+------------+
                                         |
                                         |  Provides host metrics & load status
                                         v
+-----------------------+     +------------------------+     +------------------------+
|      lsfproxyd        |<--->|       mbatchd          |<--->|        mbschd          |
| (Rate Limiter Gate)   |     | (Master Batch Daemon)  |     | (Master Scheduler)     |
+-----------------------+     +-----------+------------+     +------------------------+
                                          |
                                          |  Dispatches Job
                                          v
                              ( Execution Host Server )

[ USER COMMAND ]
  (e.g., bsub, bjobs)
       |
       v
[ Rate Limiting Gate ] ---->  lsfproxyd  
                                |
                                | (Filters/Protects mbatchd from high load)
                                v
[ Management Host ] --------> mbatchd (Master Batch Daemon)
                                |
                                |--- 1. Asks for load status ---> Master LIM
                                |                                   ^
                                |                                   | (Monitors dynamic metrics)
                                |--- 2. Requests job schedule ---> mbschd
                                |                                   |
                                |<-- 3. Returns scheduled host <----+
                                |
                                |--- 4. Dispatches job to execution host
                                v
[ Execution Host ] ---------> sbatchd (Server Batch Daemon)
                                |
                                |---> Forks: Child sbatchd
                                                |
                                                v
                                             res (Remote Execution Server)
                                                |
                                                +---> Spawns: User Job / Application
                                                |
                                                v
                                             PIM (Process Info Manager)
                                                |
                                                +---> Monitors CPU/RAM & reports back to sbatchd




LSF, daemons, daemon roles, logs, and job lifecycle
LSF architecture & daemons: LIM, RES, mbatchd, mbschd, sbatchd; log anatomy.
Environment & directories: lsf.conf, LSF_ENVDIR, LSB_CONFDIR, LSF_LOGDIR.
Start/stop/reconfigure flows: lsadmin, badmin, lsfstartup; ckconfig, reconfig, mbdrestart.
Raising debug log levels; scheduler profiling basics.
Job lifecycle, states, event/accounting files (lsb.events, lsb.acct).

----------------------------------------
Two layers, two directories:

Base layer ($LSF_CONFDIR) — describes the cluster: hosts, resources, global paths → read by lim / base daemons → reloaded with lsadmin reconfig
Batch layer ($LSB_CONFDIR/cluster/configdir) — describes workload rules: queues, limits, user shares → read by mbatchd/mbschd → reloaded with badmin reconfig
The 4 files you'll touch most often:

lsf.conf — the master anchor; wrong path = nothing starts
lsf.shared — declare custom resources (GPU, licenses) here before using them anywhere else
lsb.queues — create/modify queues, set priorities and limits
lsb.params — cluster-wide tuning (default queue, scheduling cycle, max jobs)
The golden rule: Always run ckconfig -v before reconfig — a syntax error in a live reload can destabilize daemons.


Two families of logs — different jobs:

Daemon error logs ($LSF_LOGDIR) — one file per daemon per host, written in real time. Your troubleshooting source.
Batch event/accounting logs ($LSB_SHAREDIR) — lsb.events (every job state change) and lsb.acct (finished-job resource usage). Your job history source.
Reading a log line: Every line is DATE TIME PID SEVERITY VERSION FUNCTION: message. The severity number (3=ERR, 4=WARN, 6=INFO, 7=DEBUG) is your fastest triage filter — grep for " 3 " to surface only errors instantly.

Log level rule of thumb: Default is LOG_WARNING. Raise to debug temporarily with badmin mbddebug -c all -l 2 — always revert after or logs will bloat fast.

The 5-step log triage chain: lsid → bhosts/bqueues → bjobs -p → mbatchd.log → sbatchd.log — follow it in order and you'll resolve most tickets before even opening res.log or lim.log.

Two tools, two layers:

lsadmin → controls lim + res (base layer)
badmin → controls mbatchd + sbatchd (batch layer)
The 3 reconfigure commands — know the difference:

Command	                Impact	                                                            When
badmin reconfig	    Hot-reload, zero disruption	                                            Most config changes (queues, hosts, users)
badmin mbdrestart	  Full mbatchd restart, slow on large clusters	                          Only for lsb.params changes
lsadmin reconfig	  Restarts LIM everywhere, brief pause	                                  Base config changes (lsf.shared, lsf.cluster.*)
The golden workflow: Edit → ckconfig -v → fix if errors → apply. Never skip the ckconfig step — it's the #1 thing that prevents daemon instability tickets.

bctrld is the modern replacement for the old lsadmin lim* / badmin h* subcommands. On newer fix packs, prefer it
